 3. 🏛️ House poised to impeach Hegseth 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth speaks at the Pentagon in a video last week. Image: X via Reuters House Republican leaders are bracing for enough GOP defections to impeach Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth when the measure comes to a vote this week, Axios' Kate Santaliz reports. 

 Why it matters : Republicans defeated in Tuesday's election-night bloodbath have little left to lose and could be more willing to impeach Hegseth, whose support has eroded since the start of the Iran war. Hegseth doesn't have "a lot of love" on Capitol Hill, retiring Rep. Don Bacon (R-Neb.) told Fox News. Bacon told Axios he's undecided on this week's vote. 

 Rep. Thomas Massie (R-Ky.) plans to force a vote on his resolution to impeach Hegseth for allegedly defying Congress and illegally continuing the U.S. war in Iran. The other side: Hegseth fought back with a video on X on Thursday, castigating "sham articles of impeachment" as "political theater."

 Chief Pentagon spokesman Sean Parnell said in a statement to Axios: "Secretary Hegseth will not back down or be deterred by politicians seeking to score partisan points or launch phony investigations where no wrongdoing exists. ... Secretary Hegseth is 100% committed to leading the Department until the end of President Trump's term in office." Reality check: Even if the House impeaches Hegseth, removing him would require a two-thirds vote in the Senate — a threshold that would be difficult to achieve even after Democrats take control of the chamber in January.

