 6. 🫨 Charted: Dems&#x27; youthquake 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: Preliminary results from Times Voter Trends . Chart: Arfa Momin/The New York Times Younger voters — sick of the status quo and looking for leaders closer to their own age — powered the midterm rout of the GOP.

 Why it matters: Trump's 2024 success with Gen Z voters, especially young men, fueled talk of a generational shift toward Republicans. Tuesday's results tell a different story, Axios' Russell Contreras writes . Young men swung by a stunning 39 points toward Democrats compared with '24, according to The New York Times' Voter Trends . In Texas, they swung by 50 points.

 Young women, already reliably blue voters, shifted 27 points to the left. Keep reading (gift link). 

 
 
